A||N||10||FIL||Service Engine Soon??????||||9||
Z||000000||FIL||10-31-2001||09:56 AM||||I just bought my 87 GT 2 weeks ago.  It was running beautifully, strong and stable until....<P>I was coming home from work on Monday when I noticed from the corner of my eye the "Service Engine Soon" light up for a split second then it's gone.  As soon as I saw that I felt as if the car slowed down for that time period then continued with no problem.  Then a block from my house the Service Engine light comes on again, but this time stays on.  Again as soon as the light comes on I felt the car slow down as if someone unplugged it from the outlet then plugged it back up like a fan.  What do you guys think it it?  I haven't had time to check it out, but I will on the weekend.  Do you guys think it's something major or maybe something as minor as a loose sparkplug wire?<BR>HELP HELP HELP HELP HELP HELP HELP HELP.||||reg||9
Z||000001||avengador1||10-31-2001||10:17 AM||||I had something similar happen to me on the way to work one morning. Can you still hear the fuel pump running when you first turn on the key? If not it could be the fuel pump has gone bad like mine. The mechanic told me he banged on it and got it to run again, but I had it changed anyway since it was the original one with 159,000 miles on it. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||10-31-2001||10:36 AM||||Get the codes, get the codes, get the codes...  yeah retrieve the codes, ground the diag terminals (A & B) and put the key in the on position.  Search on code 'your code goes here' and you will find out what the problem is.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||10-31-2001||11:15 AM||||First thing I would do is check the plug(s) on the ignition coil.  The little clips on them tend to break and eventually the plug starts coming off.  Happened to me a few times.  Once this plug comes off, the car will feel like it's unplugged, and yes, the check engine light will come on.<P>Other than that, you can check all other connectors, plugs and fuses.<P>After that, I would run the engine code tester.  It's easy and should give you an idea.<P>Let us know if you find the problem of if you need more help (like once you read the codes).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIL||10-31-2001||09:45 PM||||Thanks Avengador1, as a matter of fact my car has 160,000 miles on it.  I am going to look at that end.  <BR>Also I have a question for you, 87GTZ34.... were do I get a code reader that will work with my fiero and also not one that is very expensive but in the affordable range?<BR>VOYTEK, I went out this morning and pushed all the spark plug wire in making sure they were snug fit in.  I think I might of felt one loose but then again it was just my imagination.  Thanks everyone.||||reg||3
Z||000005||FIL||10-31-2001||09:47 PM||||oops VOYTEK, I ment to say "It was probably my imagination".||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||11-01-2001||12:38 AM||||FIL,  First of all welcome to PFF since I forgot to welcome you earlier.  I'm sorry your having problems right off the bat but most used Fieros have been neglected at some point in their life.  You can read codes for the price of a screwdriver and a paperclip. Yeah that's correct.  If you remove the diag plug cover which also covers the cigarett lighter, and you short pins A & B together with the car off and once shorted turn the key to the on position, the computer will flash the SES light in code.  For instance FLASH pause FLASH FLASH or better known as code '12' is the system is in diag mode.  It will flash any stored codes 3 times in a row and then flash the next stored code and repeat the whole cycle.  The codes are this web page: <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero.htm</A>  or in the Haynes, Chiltons or GM manual.  Hope this helps. GL<P>EDITED for manners<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||camon||11-01-2001||02:26 AM||||FIL...<BR>Something 87GTZ34 forgot to mention (don't mean to step on your toes 87GT......but it may be useful).<BR>The A and B pins on the ALCL are the first two pin holes on the lower row of the ALCL connector towards the passanger seat!<P>(atleast that is how mine is...not sure if they are configured same on all fiero's)<P>Just thought I would mention it incase you needed to know!<P>ohh yeah...mine is also an 87 GT (so yours should be the same configuration)!<P>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000008||FIL||11-01-2001||09:58 AM||||Guess what?...I went out this morning pushed in all the plugs and started the car to see if it would give me the same problem on startup or until the car has been running for a while.  I warmed it up went a couple of miles, pumped gas, went to the chinnese fast food place, went home, and no problem.  It ran GREAT!!!  I am so relieved, but not out of concern.  I am still going to check it out this weekend.  Once again, thanks alot guys for all the info.||||reg||4
Z||000009||87GTZ34||11-01-2001||10:51 AM||||Camon, I didn't put it in the post itself because it is spelled out on that websit with drawings and description.  Just posting cause I didn't want anyone to think I was making him guess.  BTW FIL your codes should still be stored if you haven't driven it long w/o the light coming on.  My guess is that it is the EGR switch that is giving the code '32' I believe.||||reg||1
